 "location": {
  "name": "Hạ Long Bay",
  "wiki": "Hạ_Long_Bay",
  "country": "Vietnam",
  "region": "Quảng Ninh province, Gulf of Tonkin",
  "lat": 20.9,
  "lon": 107.2,
  "tagline": "Two thousand limestone towers rising out of an emerald sea.",
  "intro": "Hạ Long Bay is a drowned karst landscape in northeast Vietnam, where well over a thousand limestone islands and islets jut straight out of the Gulf of Tonkin. UNESCO calls it the most extensive and best-known example of marine-invaded tower karst on Earth, and its grottoes, floating villages and junk-rigged cruise boats make it Vietnam's signature sight.",
  "stats": [
   {
    "label": "Bay area",
    "value": "≈1,553 km² (600 sq mi)"
   },
   {
    "label": "Islands & islets",
    "value": "1,133 in the UNESCO site"
   },
   {
    "label": "Limestone age",
    "value": "≈500 million years"
   },
   {
    "label": "UNESCO World Heritage",
    "value": "Since 1994 (Cat Ba added 2023)"
   }
  ],
  "facts": [
   "Hạ Long means \"descending dragon\": legend says the gods sent a family of dragons to defend Vietnam, and the jewels they spat out became the islands.",
   "Around 1,600 people live right on the bay in four floating fishing villages, in homes on rafts buoyed by tires and plastic jugs.",
   "Late-19th-century French visitors left graffiti inside Hang Đầu Gỗ, the largest grotto in the Hạ Long area.",
   "People have lived here for roughly 20,000 years: the prehistoric Soi Nhụ culture dates to about 18,000–7,000 BC.",
